\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Example}
\title{The Lorentzian four-dimensional Spin group is #{SL_2(\mathbb C)}}

\p{For #{Q_{1,3}=x_0^2-x_1^2-x_2^2-x_3^2}, put
#{A_j=e_0e_j} for #{1\le j\le3} and #{I=e_0e_1e_2e_3}. Then the #{A_j}
square to #{1} and anticommute, while

##{I^2=-1,\qquad A_1A_2A_3=-I.}

For the Pauli matrices

##{\sigma_1=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
\sigma_2=\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\i&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
\sigma_3=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},}

the assignments #{\Psi(A_j)=\sigma_j} define a real-algebra map from the
even Clifford algebra to #{M_2(\mathbb C)}. Since
#{\sigma_1\sigma_2\sigma_3=i1}, necessarily #{\Psi(I)=-i1}. The images of
#{1,A_1,A_2,A_3,I,IA_1,IA_2,IA_3} form a real basis, so #{\Psi} is an
algebra equivalence.}

\p{Write a matrix uniquely as

##{M=z_0 1+z_1\sigma_1+z_2\sigma_2+z_3\sigma_3,
\qquad z_j=a_j+i b_j,}

where

##{\begin{aligned}
z_0&=(m_{11}+m_{22})/2,&z_3&=(m_{11}-m_{22})/2,\\
z_1&=(m_{12}+m_{21})/2,&z_2&=(m_{21}-m_{12})/(2i).
\end{aligned}}

Then an explicit inverse is

##{\Psi^{-1}(M)
=a_0+\sum_{j=1}^3a_jA_j
-I\left(b_0+\sum_{j=1}^3b_jA_j\right).}

Reversal fixes #{I} and negates every #{A_j}; under #{\Psi} it is the
complex-linear matrix adjugate. Hence

##{\Psi(\operatorname{rev}(x))=\operatorname{adj}(\Psi(x)),\qquad
\Psi(\operatorname{rev}(x)x)=\det(\Psi(x))1.}

For an arbitrary even #{x}, this norm is algebra-valued: the determinant may
be complex, corresponding to the even center. It is not yet the real
scalar-valued Lipschitz norm, and adjugation is not conjugate transpose.}

\p{Restriction therefore gives a homomorphism
#{\Spin(Q_{1,3})\to SL_2(\mathbb C)}. Conversely, a determinant-one matrix
has adjugate as a two-sided inverse. Its displayed Clifford preimage is an
even unit with #{\operatorname{rev}(x)x=1}, so the dimension-four carrier
lemma places it in Spin. The written inverse proves both compositions and
multiplicativity:

##{\Spin(Q_{1,3})\simeq SL_2(\mathbb C).}}

\p{The source indices require translation.
\citet{I.3, Theorem 3.7, pp. 23--24}{lawson2016spin}
use #{v^2=-q(v)} and give the even-algebra reduction

##{\operatorname{Cl}^{0}_{3,1}\simeq\operatorname{Cl}_{2,1}.}

Their base entries and recurrence then give

##{\operatorname{Cl}_{2,1}\simeq M_2(\mathbb C)}

\citet{I.4, (4.0) and Theorem 4.1, p. 25}{lawson2016spin}.}

\p{Gallier uses the opposite indexing. The positive-square #{A_j} exhibit
#{\operatorname{Cl}_{3,0}}.
\citet{Example 1.3, p. 33}{gallier2014clifford}
records the matrix base entries, while
\citet{Lemma 1.15, p. 34}{gallier2014clifford}
supplies the recurrence. Together they give

##{\operatorname{Cl}_{3,0}
\simeq\operatorname{Cl}_{0,1}\otimes\operatorname{Cl}_{2,0}
\simeq M_2(\mathbb C).}}

\p{\citet{Chapter 17, Proposition 17.5, pp. 151--152}{porteous1995clifford}
identifies his norm-one subgroup #{\Spin^{+}(1,3)} with #{SL_2(\mathbb C)}.
He computes reversal as adjugation.
His broader mixed-sign carrier permits norm #{\pm1}, and his displayed axes
are not the Pauli axes fixed above.}

\p{Here the even center is #{\mathbb R[I]\simeq\mathbb C}, with no nonzero
complementary central idempotents. This contrasts with the split compact
center #{\mathbb R\oplus\mathbb R}, although both even algebras have real
dimension eight. The full algebra #{\Cl_{1,3}\simeq M_2(\mathbb H)} in
\ref{fcap-0026} is a different algebra. The vector-action image remains the
spinor-norm kernel from \ref{fcap-0023}; no topological component or concrete
gamma-map claim is made.}
